  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nSatellite imagery showed a more zonal component of the mid to upper \nlevel winds drawing the moist plume in a more northeasterly \ndirection into eastern Utah and Western Colorado. The 12Z model runs \nhave picked up on this shift moving the Pacific Northwest low \nfarther inland to knock the top off the ridge to the east over the \nPlains. Look for this shift to direct the moist plume into the Four \nCorners area and north into the Uncompahgre with pwats around an \ninch. Like wise, the jet has a stronger zonal component bringing \nsome upper level support to the region late Sunday into Monday. Most \nguidance hasn't adjusted to this shift, but the HRRR and SREF \nseems to track on this. Blended in 10-20% of these to get at \nleast isolated showers and thunderstorms into the forecast \nSunday afternoon over the higher terrain across the southern \nand central areas. Convection in this plume of moisture is \ncapably of producing stronger thunderstorms with periods of \nheavy rain that can lead to flash flooding, especially near \nrecent fire scars and in areas prone to flooding. Due to the \npoor run-to-run consistency of the models, confidence in the \nforecast is too low at this point to issue any Watch products \nat this time. Stay tuned over the next 12 hours for updates as \nthe models come more into line, and stay aware to the weather \naround you if you're going out. \n\nBeyond Monday, models are in agreement on the high pressure over \nthe Southern Plains working west over the Desert Southwest and into \nIntermountain West shutting off the monsoonal tap, but guidance \nkeeps afternoon showers and thunderstorms in the forecast over the \nhigher terrain mostly from residual moisture in the area. \nTemperatures today are running around normal across much of the \nregion to about five degrees above normal across the southern areas. \nLook for temperatures to warm to about five to ten degrees above \nnormal by the late next week.",
